narrative:
## Safeguarding Every Increment

A counter has three points of contact: the value, the minimum, and the maximum. The value must stay within bounds. This is a perfect use case for invariants plus requires:

```xiom
type Counter = {
  value: Int;
  min: Int;
  max: Int;
  invariant: value >= min;
  invariant: value <= max;
}

fn Counter.increment(self: &mut Self)
  requires: self.value < self.max;
  ensures: self.value == self@pre.value + 1;
{
  self.value = self.value + 1;
}

fn Counter.decrement(self: &mut Self)
  requires: self.value > self.min;
  ensures: self.value == self@pre.value - 1;
{
  self.value = self.value - 1;
}
```

The `requires` clauses prevent callers from incrementing past max or decrementing below min. The `ensures` clauses prove by exactly how much the value changed. If a caller tries to increment at max, the contract traps BEFORE any mutation occurs:

```xiom
fn Counter.reset(self: &mut Self)
  ensures: self.value == self.min;
{
  self.value = self.min;
}
```

Reset always works — no contract needed because `self.min` is, by definition, a valid value for the counter. The invariant (value >= min) guarantees it.

